---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import SheetDownload from '../../components/SheetDownload.astro';

/**
 * Marketing research 2026-11-04, published 2026-11-05.
 *
 * Program landing page, same shape as stronglifts-5x5-tracker.astro: what the
 * built-in routine does, what the lifter still decides, how history comes over.
 * Every program fact below was read from public/programs/gzclp.badger (exported
 * by the app, app_version 1.1.15 on 2026-10-23), not from memory. Re-check it
 * against that file whenever the gallery is regenerated.
 *
 * GZCLP is Cody Lefever's program. The page describes compatibility and says
 * nothing about affiliation, as the 5x5 page does.
 */

const description =
  'Run GZCLP in Badger: the T1, T2 and T3 stage ladders are written as rules, so a missed lift moves to its next stage and says so. What the template does and what you still decide.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is there a GZCLP app that moves the stages for me?',
    a: 'Yes. Badger ships GZCLP with the T1 and T2 stage ladders written as progression rules. When you miss a lift, the next session proposes the next stage (for T1, 5x3, then 6x2, then 10x1) and shows the line that fired. You can type over any proposed weight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does the Badger GZCLP template include the AMRAP sets?',
    a: 'Yes. The last set of every T1 stage, and of T3, is marked as a set to take as far as you can. On T3 the weight goes up once that set reaches 25 reps.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I get the GZCLP routine?',
    a: 'It is in the app under You, Routines, in the Intermediate Programs folder. You can also download gzclp.badger from the programs page and open it on your phone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I bring my history from Strong, Hevy or FitNotes?',
    a: 'Yes. Badger imports CSV exports from Strong, Hevy, FitNotes and StrongLifts, then you set the GZCLP routine active.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a GZCLP spreadsheet?',
    a: 'Yes. Download the GZCLP spreadsheet as Excel or CSV from this page. It lists the T1, T2 and T3 stages and works out each week\'s weights from your starting numbers, rounded to your plates.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does Badger cost?',
    a: 'Nothing. There is no account and no cap on routines or history.',
  },
];
---

<HelpLayout title="GZCLP app and tracker" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="GZCLP in Badger"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-11-05"
    about={['GZCLP', 'Linear progression']}
    breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' }]}
  />
  <script
    type="application/ld+json"
    slot="head"
    set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'FAQPage',
      mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: f.q,
        acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
      })),
    })}
  />

  <h1>GZCLP in Badger</h1>

  <p>
    Badger runs GZCLP and keeps the stage bookkeeping for you. Miss a lift and the next session
    proposes the next stage, with the rule that fired shown above the sets. This page covers what
    the built-in routine contains, what you still decide, and how to bring your history across.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-in-the-box">What the GZCLP routine contains</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Four days, three tiers each.</strong> Day 1 is squat (T1), bench (T2) and lat
      pulldown (T3). Day 2 is overhead press, deadlift and dumbbell row. Day 3 is bench, squat and
      lat pulldown. Day 4 is deadlift, overhead press and dumbbell row. The days are set for Monday,
      Tuesday, Thursday and Friday, and you can move them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>T1 stages.</strong> Five sets of three, then six sets of two, then ten sets of one.
      The last set of each stage is an as-many-as-you-can set. Miss any set and the lift moves to
      the next stage. Missing at the last stage restarts it at 5x3 with 10% off the weight.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>T2 stages.</strong> Three sets of ten, then three of eight, then three of six, with
      the same move-on-a-miss rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>T3.</strong> Three sets of fifteen with the last set as many as you can. The weight
      goes up when that set reaches 25 reps.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Step sizes.</strong> The routine adds 5 kg to squat and deadlift and 2.5 kg to bench
      and press when every set is met. Tap any number in a rule to change it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-you-decide">What you still decide</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Starting weights.</strong> The routine carries the rep scheme only. Enter what you
      lift in the first session; after that Badger proposes each weight from the last one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every proposal is editable.</strong> The preview shows the weight and the reason, for
      example the stage a lift is on. Type your own weight and the rule keeps running. See
      <a href="/help/progression/#rules">progression rules</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Accessories.</strong> The T3 slots are a starting point. Swap them in the routine
      editor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">The short version</span>
    <p>
      Open the routine, enter your starting weights, and train. Badger tracks each lift's stage and
      proposes the next session.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="get-it">Get the routine</h2>

  <p>
    In the app, go to <strong>You &rarr; Routines</strong> and open the Intermediate Programs folder.
    Or download <code>gzclp.badger</code> from the <a href="/programs/">programs page</a> and open it
    on your phone. The file is plain text you can read first; the
    <a href="/badger-format/">format is documented</a>.
  </p>

  <SheetDownload slug="gzclp" name="GZCLP" inputs="starting weights" />

  <h2 id="import">Bringing your history over</h2>

  <p>
    Export a CSV from Strong, Hevy, FitNotes or StrongLifts, then use <strong>Settings &rarr; Backup,
    import and export &rarr; Import from another app</strong>. Steps and what each importer carries
    are in <a href="/guides/switching/">switching from another app</a>. Import the history first, set
    the GZCLP routine active after.
  </p>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>

  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    GZCLP was created by Cody Lefever. Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by or connected to him.
    This page describes a routine written for Badger from the publicly known shape of the program.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
